          %title Observables
          %h2#Observables Observables
          %p In this chapter we discuss these topics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#GeneralObservables"} General observables
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#LinearOffShellObservablesAreDistributions"} Polynomial off-shell observables and Distributions
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#PolynomialOnShellObservablesAreDistributionalSolutionsToTheEquationsOfMotion"} Polynomial on-shell observables and Distributional solutions to PDEs
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#LocalObservablesByTransgression"} Local observables and Transgression
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#InfinitesimalObservables"} Infinitesimal observables
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#StatesArePositiveLinearFunctionals"} States
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            (def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianForFreeScalarFieldOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
              quantity
            or just
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            for short (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Observable"}
            below), is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mi EL
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi L
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \;\colon\; \Gamma_\Sigma(E)_{\delta_{EL}\mathbf{L} = 0} \longrightarrow \mathbb{C}
          %p
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            (example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SupergeometricSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional"}> functional
            ” of field histories. We think of this as assigning to each physically realizable field history
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            the value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(\Phi)
            of the given quantity as exhibited by that field history. For instance concepts like “average
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            in the compact spacetime region
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %em
              field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amplitude"} amplitude
              at spacetime point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            should be an observable, the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observable"}> field observable
            ” denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Φ
                      %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
          %p
            Beware that in much of the literature on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
            (example below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PointEvaluationObservables"}
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                of fields/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundles"} field bundles
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"}> field histories
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Φ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Φ
                          %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
          %p
            In particular, the process of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            (discussed in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Quantization"} Quantization
            below) affects the third of these concepts only, in that it
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deforms
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on observables to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+algebra"} non-commutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
            are often referred to as
            = succeed "." do
              %em quantum fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            it is important that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
            are really the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th aspect
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                %th description
                %th def.
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
