          %p#LinearOffShellObservablesAreDistributions
            %strong Polynomial off-shell Observables and Distributions
          %p
            We consider here
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+observables"} linear observables
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LinearObservables"}
            below) and more generally
            %em quadratic observables
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuadraticObservables"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+observables"} polynomial observables
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PolynomialObservables"}
            below) for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
            and discuss how these are equivalently given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            against
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
            called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LinearObservablesAreTheCompactlySupportedDistributions"}
            and prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DistributionsAreGeneralizedFunctions"}
            below).
          %p
            This is the basis for the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} further below
          %p
